WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I hired a opposition
research company

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
to go in and pull the
voter registration records

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:07.407
of the White House press corps.

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:09.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
These are public records,
anybody can do it.

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:13.513
And they discovered that
by a ratio of 12 to one,

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:15.048
the seats of the White House

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:17.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
are occupied by
registered Democrats.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.253
(soft upbeat music)

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:26.059 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:28.495 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
is a former White
House press secretary,

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:31.431 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
a Fox News contributor and
author of the new book,

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:32.632
I've got in my hand right now,

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:36.269 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Suppression, Deception,
Snobbery, and Bias:

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:40.340
"Why the Press Gets So Much
Wrong and Just Doesn't Care.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.842
Ari Fleischer, welcome
to the "Rubin Report".

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:44.411
- Dave, thank you for having me.

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:49.015
- Ari, I feel like you wrote
this book for me personally

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:52.552
because this basically gets
to the heart of everything

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:56.689
that I do every hour that I
spend working on this show

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:59.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the media is sort
of a bigger problem

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.694
than almost anything
else at the moment.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.563 align:start size:81% position:19%
But before we dive
into the book,

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:07.333
you have a really interesting
backstory and all that stuff.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
How do you get
involved in politics?

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:10.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
What eventually got
you to the White House

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.605
and then we'll get
into all this stuff?

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.875 align:start position:0% line:0%
- Well, to put it in a nutshell,
it was totally an accident.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.410 line:0%
I graduated from college.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.213 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
I moved home to play
baseball and a friend of mine

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:22.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
had a brother who was
managing a congressional race.

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.818
They needed a press secretary.

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:28.488
I knew nothing and I got
the job which tells you

00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:31.591
how little you need to know
to be a press secretary.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.626 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I fell in love
with politics.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:36.463
We lost, it was 1982.

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:39.265
Reagan stayed the course.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
Only one Republican
challenger won that cycle

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:44.437
but I got the bug, I
moved to Washington.

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:46.906
I was unemployed and I got
a job as a press secretary

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:50.610
to a New York congressman and
that began a 16 year career

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.346 align:start size:88% position:13%
on Capitol Hill followed
by the Bush campaign

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:56.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
where I moved to Texas in
1999 and then The White House.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:02:01.688 align:start position:0%
- Do you miss being in the
thick of the political madness?

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:02.522
- No.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.125
- Nothing?

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:06.726
Is there nothing
you miss about it?

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:09.262 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I spent 21 wonderful
years in Washington

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.431
and I loved every one
of the days I was there

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:12.866
and especially at
The White House

00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:14.734 align:start size:88% position:13%
and when I was
communications director

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.002
of the Ways and Means Committee

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.071
after Republican took
control of the house,

00:02:18.071 --> 00:02:21.407 align:start size:84% position:16%
tremendous excitement
and satisfaction

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:23.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I was never a
Washington for life type.

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:26.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
So one day you just
know, your day has come,

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:27.347
it's time to go.

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:30.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it hit me after
21 long years.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.453 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I loved it, I loved
standing at that podium Dave,

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.955
at The White House,
I loved taking heat.

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
I loved catching the curve
balls, the fast balls

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.127
and standing my ground
on behalf of a president

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:43.530
and a person who I
really believe in.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.764
And that's what it's about.

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.266
If you believe deeply,

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:49.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
Washington is the town to
be in and then go back home.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.273
- Believe it or not, I
watched many, many, many

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:55.909 align:start size:97% position:3%
of those press conferences
that you were doing back then.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:58.745
They were sort of must see TV
and this will sort of segue us

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.547
a little bit bit to the book,

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:04.184 align:start size:88% position:13%
Correct me if I'm
wrong, the media treats

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:07.487
Republican press secretaries
and Republican administrations

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:10.490
a little bit different than
Democrat press secretaries

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.292
and administrations,
is that fair to say?

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:14.994
- Yeah, I just caught
friendly softballs

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:16.496 align:start size:81% position:19%
from The White
House press corps,

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:18.498 align:start position:0%
you know they loved
conservatives, they loved Bush.

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:21.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look, the truth is as much
as things have changed

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.936 align:start size:84% position:16%
since I was press
secretary 20 years ago

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:24.771
and they have changed a lot,

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:27.307
they've gotten a lot
worse and nastier,

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:31.044 align:start size:88% position:13%
fact is right now, Peter
Doocy from Fox News,

00:03:31.044 --> 00:03:35.148
the only one of those 49
seats asking hard questions

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.016
to the Biden press secretary.

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:38.451
When I was press secretary,

00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
there were 49 seats
asking me hard questions.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:43.856
That's the problem,
the press takes sides

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.592
and they did back then.

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:47.060
But back then, David,

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was more just your
liberal conservative bias

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:51.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
reporters still thought their
job at the end of the day

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:54.500
was be objective and
tell the news fair

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.570
and not give their
opinions on the air.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.540
Today you watch CNN, you
watch almost every show

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.675
and they think their
job is to give opinions

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.378
and that's what they do and
it's killing journalism.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.880
- So when do you
think that started?

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because I think anyone
watching this, they get it.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:12.885
That's what I spend most of
my show doing at this point

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:17.257
is debunking a lot
of the nonsense that
CNN, New York Times,

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:19.759 align:start size:97% position:3%
Washington Post, that
they're putting out as fact,

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.094
when if you just
read the headline

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:24.163 align:start size:88% position:13%
with a little more
precision, you will see

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:25.832
that it actually is opinion.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:27.533
- Yeah, absolutely right.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:29.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
Look, it started
because of two reasons.

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:30.870
One is technology.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.673
The internet led to the
decline of advertising

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:37.210
for print newspapers and it
changed how you can watch

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.646 align:start size:94% position:6%
the news as we had a
proliferation of outlets.

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:41.914 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you had conservatives
watching conservative media,

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.050 align:start size:84% position:16%
you had liberals
watching liberal media

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.286 align:start size:81% position:19%
and newspapers
had no more money.

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:50.023
The only way they got money
was from their subscribers.

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.592
And so, they didn't wanna
alienate their subscribers.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:55.428 align:start size:97% position:3%
Unlike an advertiser, who
wanted you to appeal broadly

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:57.830 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the entire country
so you had to be mindful

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
of staying somewhat
sensible and accurate,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.568
now you could let it rip.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.537
And then along came
more social media,

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.572 align:start size:88% position:13%
Twitter and other places
where reporters felt

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.508
really good given
their opinions.

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:10.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
They didn't value
objectivity anymore,

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:12.378
they wanted them more
Twitter followers.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:15.648 align:start size:97% position:3%
They wanted the praise of
Hollywood actors and actresses

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.183
when they let their opinion rip.

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:21.688 align:start size:88% position:13%
Hence Jim Acosta at CNN,
John Harwood at CNN,

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:26.092 align:start size:88% position:13%
daytime reporters who
don't report, they opine

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.394
and always from a far
left point of view,

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:30.897
anti-Trump, anti-Republican.

00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:32.832
And then the other
factor was Trump.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:37.170 align:start size:97% position:3%
When Trump emerged, the press
somehow made the decision

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.005
to save the republic.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:43.042 align:start size:97% position:3%
They decided that the
American people erred in 2016

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:44.711
by not electing Hillary

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:47.113 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the press was
gonna fix the mistake.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:49.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
So these snobs put
themselves in the position

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.718
superior to the people and said,

00:05:51.718 --> 00:05:53.586
collusion is probably
how come Trump won.

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:55.088
The Steele dossier is credible.

00:05:55.088 --> 00:05:57.390
And we're gonna put
Trump and the country

00:05:57.390 --> 00:06:00.893
through years of misery to
make the case against him.

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:02.962
And as I write in the book,

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:05.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
they didn't put just
their finger on the scale,

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:06.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
they put their writing
hand on the scale

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:09.535
and many of 'em put their
entire bodies on the scale.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.838 align:start size:88% position:13%
And this is the
mainstream media today.

00:06:11.838 --> 00:06:13.139 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's why I
wrote the book, David,

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:15.675
this is why I blew the
whistle on the press.

00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:17.443
I try to be fair.

00:06:17.443 --> 00:06:19.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
I will always call
the balls and strikes.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.748 align:start size:84% position:16%
I supported President
Trump on policy

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:23.816
pretty much all the time.

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:26.719
I regularly tweeted that I
objected to his behavior.

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:28.821
When he tweeted something
I thought went too far

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:31.657
and was offensive,
I called him on it.

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:34.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
But when I saw the
coverage of Donald Trump,

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:36.729
how biased, unfair, get Trump.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.932
If it was anti-Trump,
it got a bump coverage.

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:41.667
I said, I'm gonna
write about this.

00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:43.403
It's not good for the country.

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:44.871
It's not good for democracy.

00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:47.874
It's not good for journalism
when reporters are in the tank.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.878 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you think that they
would've done the same thing

00:06:51.878 --> 00:06:53.980
to your guy, George W. Bush,

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
had social media been
as big back then?

00:06:56.382 --> 00:06:58.217
'Cause I remember very
specifically one day,

00:06:58.217 --> 00:06:59.852
I used to live on the upper west

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:02.922 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is bastion of
liberalism, Columbia AU.

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:04.891
And I remember during
the Bush presidency

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:06.192
and it was during the Iraq war

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:08.127 align:start size:88% position:13%
when the media was
going crazy all the time

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:11.898
and you were doing the press
junkets and all that stuff.

00:07:11.898 --> 00:07:14.367 line:0%
I remember walking out and
there was a guy with a table

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:17.170 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
with books and I remember
every single book

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:19.639
basically was George
W. Bush is the devil

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:21.707
like every single book.

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:23.609 align:start size:88% position:13%
And they were all
being bought of course.

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:25.745
And had social media
existed at the time,

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:27.547
my guess is they would've
done the exact same thing.

00:07:27.547 --> 00:07:28.781
It just would've
happened earlier.

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:30.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I know you're
right about that.

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:32.418
And I think one of the
points I make in the book

00:07:32.418 --> 00:07:35.254 align:start size:97% position:3%
is regardless of what
President Trump decides to do

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:36.589
whether he runs or doesn't run,

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:38.224
they're gonna do
it to Ron DeSantis.

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:39.525
They're gonna do
it to Kristi Noem.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:42.161
They're gonna do it to
Ted Cruz or Tom Cotton.

00:07:42.161 --> 00:07:45.598
And here's where I have some
sympathy for President Trump.

00:07:45.598 --> 00:07:48.734 align:start size:84% position:16%
What he will tell you
is Republicans don't
fight hard enough

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:51.337
and that's why he
does what he does.

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:54.607
And I think there's some merit
to that because he recognized

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:57.009
the mainstream media is in the
tank, they're out to get 'em,

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.878 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're not fair,
they're not objective.

00:07:58.878 --> 00:08:02.381
And so it made him fight twice
as hard, three times as hard.

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:04.750
I wish sometimes he
had just gotten it

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.886
with a little more
subtlety or nuance

00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:08.287
but that's not his nature.

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:11.123 align:start size:84% position:16%
He let it rip
everywhere he went.

00:08:11.123 --> 00:08:12.658
He was a bull in a China shop

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:15.361
and he took a new China shop
with him everywhere he went.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:19.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, it's a calibration
issue but yeah,

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
Republicans and
conservatives need to fight

00:08:21.968 --> 00:08:24.270 align:start size:84% position:16%
because my conclusion
writing this book

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.572
is that the media has
turned to activists.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:29.342 align:start position:0%
The mainstream media and by
that I mean the New York Times,

00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:33.279
The Washington Post,
ABC, NBC, CBS, CNN,

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:36.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
they are activists for a cause
and it's a liberal cause.

00:08:36.716 --> 00:08:38.851
My first chapter is
called original sin

00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:42.655
and I go into how many
reporters are Democrats?

00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:46.025 align:start size:84% position:16%
I hired a opposition
research company

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:48.427 align:start size:94% position:6%
to go in and pull the
voter registration records

00:08:48.427 --> 00:08:49.829
of The White House Press Corp.

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:52.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
These are public records,
anybody can do it.

00:08:52.365 --> 00:08:56.502
And they discovered that
by a ratio of 12 to one,

00:08:56.502 --> 00:08:58.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
the seats of The White
House are occupied

00:08:58.104 --> 00:08:59.539
by registered Democrats.

00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:02.341 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, Dave, why
isn't it one to one?

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:05.244
Or why isn't it 12 to one
Republican to Democrat?

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:09.248 align:start size:97% position:3%
The news would be different
if it was 12 to one

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:11.050
Republican to Democrat.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:12.585
But The White House press corp,

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:14.186
journalism schools
throughout America,

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.289
newsrooms pretty much
throughout America,

00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:18.724 align:start size:88% position:13%
but especially in
Washington and New York

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.960
overwhelmingly cut
from the same cloth.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:25.698
Think alike, act alike, dress
alike, tweet alike Democrats.

00:09:25.698 --> 00:09:29.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
And there's another
study I found in here.

00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:31.170
It's a Pew Research study.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.272
The only group of
Americans who say

00:09:33.272 --> 00:09:37.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
the press understands them are
college educated Democrats.

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:39.145
If you're a Democrat-

00:09:39.145 --> 00:09:40.446
- You don't say?

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:42.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You say the press
doesn't understand me.

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:43.883 align:start size:84% position:16%
Independents,
regardless of degree,

00:09:43.883 --> 00:09:45.084
press doesn't understand me

00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:46.786 align:start size:84% position:16%
and certainly all
Republicans say that.

00:09:46.786 --> 00:09:49.188 align:start size:88% position:13%
The press is dug itself
into a cul-de-sac.

00:09:49.188 --> 00:09:51.991 align:start position:0%
They are college educated
Democrats who know how to write

00:09:51.991 --> 00:09:55.261
and think only for fellow
college educated Democrats.

00:09:55.261 --> 00:09:56.462
And if you own a gun,

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:58.631 align:start size:88% position:13%
if your grandfather
taught you how to hunt,

00:09:58.631 --> 00:10:00.466 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you think life
begins a conception,

00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:04.470
if you pray every day, the
press doesn't understand you.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:06.939 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's a huge
problem with journalism.

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:10.476
- Do you think that they
know that they're biased?

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:11.911
I mean, I think you're right

00:10:11.911 --> 00:10:13.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you said the
Twitter thing before.

00:10:13.446 --> 00:10:16.182
Twitter sort of unasked it
because then every minute

00:10:16.182 --> 00:10:18.050
of every day they were
sharing their opinions

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then over time, you
could aggregate it and go,

00:10:20.319 --> 00:10:22.788 align:start size:97% position:3%
okay, you guys obviously
have a certain point of view

00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:24.023
but I look at a guy like,

00:10:24.023 --> 00:10:26.692
you know I hit CNN all
the time on my show.

00:10:26.692 --> 00:10:29.629
Jake Tapper is always thought
of as the one non-insane guy

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:31.864 align:start size:84% position:16%
there but it's obvious
he's a Democrat.

00:10:31.864 --> 00:10:34.400 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like do you think that they
know what they're doing

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.736 align:start size:84% position:16%
or do you think they
honestly believe

00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:38.270
they're pulling the
wool over our eyes?

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:41.073
- They know it and I've
talked to 'em about it.

00:10:41.073 --> 00:10:43.576
They know it, a lot of 'em
privately acknowledge it

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.278
but they won't do
anything about it.

00:10:46.278 --> 00:10:48.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is the problem 'cause
they're too like-minded.

00:10:48.214 --> 00:10:50.182
They're in denial and
they're in decline.

00:10:50.182 --> 00:10:51.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're watching their
viewership go down,

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:53.619 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're watching
their ratings go down.

00:10:53.619 --> 00:10:56.422
They're watching the trust from
the American people go down.

00:10:56.422 --> 00:10:59.025 align:start size:94% position:6%
But unlike any
corporation who would say

00:10:59.025 --> 00:11:01.894
when they have a trend like
that, what's wrong with us?

00:11:01.894 --> 00:11:03.596 align:start size:81% position:19%
What do we need
to do differently?

00:11:03.596 --> 00:11:06.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
Reporters tend to say,
what's wrong with America?

00:11:06.365 --> 00:11:08.334
What do the Americans
need to do differently?

00:11:08.334 --> 00:11:09.368
- So what do we do about that?

00:11:09.368 --> 00:11:10.736
What do we do about that?

00:11:10.736 --> 00:11:13.172 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, is it that
basically the new guy at CNN

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:14.507
just has to fire everybody.

00:11:14.507 --> 00:11:15.941
I mean, is it as simple as that?

00:11:15.941 --> 00:11:17.143
Does it even matter anymore?

00:11:17.143 --> 00:11:18.444
I mean, should any of us care?

00:11:18.444 --> 00:11:20.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
We know that the
ratings are going down.

00:11:20.112 --> 00:11:21.881
Should we just let them die?

00:11:21.881 --> 00:11:23.482
Let them just go extinct?

00:11:23.482 --> 00:11:25.117
- No and here's where I think,

00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:26.719
and I hope my book is
a little bit different.

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:29.288
I point these things out
because I lament them.

00:11:29.288 --> 00:11:32.224
I want the press to be fair,
I want them to be objective.

00:11:32.224 --> 00:11:34.427 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's so good for America
if we can pick up

00:11:34.427 --> 00:11:37.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
one source of news and say,
yep, I believe it, I trust it.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.433
Now here's what I think
needs to be done about it

00:11:40.433 --> 00:11:43.903
without the media telling us
what we're supposed to think.

00:11:43.903 --> 00:11:46.972 align:start position:0%
My solution to it is number
one, young people conservatives

00:11:46.972 --> 00:11:49.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
need to get into
journalism, it's healthy.

00:11:49.675 --> 00:11:54.213 align:start position:0%
Number two, journalism schools,
newsrooms need to actively

00:11:54.213 --> 00:11:56.515
recruit people who
think differently.

00:11:56.515 --> 00:12:00.086
Journalism needs a booster
shot of independent thought

00:12:00.086 --> 00:12:01.754
and that's not gonna come
from the same old people.

00:12:01.754 --> 00:12:04.857 align:start size:97% position:3%
It will come from more
conservative thinking people.

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:06.559
So if I'm a journalism school,

00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:08.160 align:start size:84% position:16%
if I'm a Columbia
School of Journalism,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.296 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I interview
somebody, I would say,

00:12:10.296 --> 00:12:11.297
tell me what your habits are,

00:12:11.297 --> 00:12:12.765
what are your favorite hobbies?

00:12:12.765 --> 00:12:16.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
And if somebody says, I like
to hunt, I like to fish,

00:12:16.302 --> 00:12:18.471
I enjoy going to church.

00:12:18.471 --> 00:12:21.173 align:start size:88% position:13%
To me, that is a sign
of ideological diversity

00:12:21.173 --> 00:12:23.676
that I want in my newsroom.

00:12:23.676 --> 00:12:27.279
These people are so hung up on
sexual orientation or gender

00:12:27.279 --> 00:12:31.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
or race as opposed to the
most powerful diversity of all

00:12:31.317 --> 00:12:34.720
which is diversity of the
mind, the way you think.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.523 align:start position:0%
So that's number one, journalism
schools need to change.

00:12:37.523 --> 00:12:39.492
Conservatives need
to go into J school.

00:12:39.492 --> 00:12:42.828
And then newsrooms, and this
is where the CNN experiment

00:12:42.828 --> 00:12:44.296
is gonna be fascinating.

00:12:44.296 --> 00:12:46.132
The new leadership of CNN
says they wanna return

00:12:46.132 --> 00:12:48.267
to objectivity, I'm
gonna watch them.

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:50.936
I wanna see what
they do, we'll see.

00:12:50.936 --> 00:12:52.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So on the first
point on the schools,

00:12:52.872 --> 00:12:56.142
I mean, I agree with you but
do you think there's honestly

00:12:56.142 --> 00:12:59.845
any chance in high hell that
anyone at Columbia University

00:12:59.845 --> 00:13:02.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's been completely
infected by this woke thing

00:13:02.448 --> 00:13:03.716
is gonna do it?

00:13:03.716 --> 00:13:06.018
I mean, do you think any
professor of journalism

00:13:06.018 --> 00:13:07.453
is gonna put their
butt on the line

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:08.888
to do what you just
said right there?

00:13:08.888 --> 00:13:10.589
- Yeah, probably not.

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:13.359
And again, I lament it
but that's the solution

00:13:13.359 --> 00:13:14.794
'cause what's the alternative?

00:13:14.794 --> 00:13:16.896 align:start size:97% position:3%
The alternative as we
continue the trend we're on,

00:13:16.896 --> 00:13:19.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now here's what that
trend looks like.

00:13:19.098 --> 00:13:21.300
Conservative media is booming.

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:23.969 align:start size:88% position:13%
Conservative viewership
continues to go up.

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:26.205
Liberal media, it goes down

00:13:26.205 --> 00:13:29.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's not healthy
for democracy.

00:13:29.208 --> 00:13:32.044
Again, I want to be able to
pick up one source of news

00:13:32.044 --> 00:13:34.313
and say, I'm done for the
day, it took me 10 minutes.

00:13:34.313 --> 00:13:37.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
I read it, I accept it, and
here's what we need to do now.

00:13:37.583 --> 00:13:39.618
And then you debate it
in the political sphere

00:13:39.618 --> 00:13:42.021
and you actually go to
vote and whoever wins,

00:13:42.021 --> 00:13:44.290
wins the election and
moves the country.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.726
But we shouldn't have to as
a people read five sources

00:13:47.726 --> 00:13:50.496
of news to figure out what
is right and what is wrong

00:13:50.496 --> 00:13:52.264
and weigh it and balance it.

00:13:52.264 --> 00:13:53.732 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I get that
question all the time.

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.000
Where can I go?

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
And frankly, I'll tell
you this too, Dave,

00:13:56.769 --> 00:13:58.704
the best show on the
news in my opinion

00:13:58.704 --> 00:14:01.874
is the six o'clock evening
show on Fox with Bret Baier.

00:14:01.874 --> 00:14:04.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is old fashioned,
straight down the middle,

00:14:04.243 --> 00:14:05.778
good reporting.

00:14:05.778 --> 00:14:08.314
It's not cut from the same
liberal cloth but it's not

00:14:08.314 --> 00:14:10.616 align:start size:88% position:13%
ideological conservative
show either.

00:14:10.616 --> 00:14:13.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just straight
reporting and I miss that,

00:14:13.452 --> 00:14:14.954
I like watching that show.

00:14:14.954 --> 00:14:17.223
- So speaking of Fox since
you're a Fox contributor

00:14:17.223 --> 00:14:18.991 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I go on Fox a
couple times a week.

00:14:18.991 --> 00:14:21.160
I find Fox, they never
tell me what to say,

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.495
I say whatever I want, often
things that are against say

00:14:23.495 --> 00:14:26.198
the general conservative line.

00:14:27.666 --> 00:14:29.068
I'm guessing that they let
you say whatever you want

00:14:29.068 --> 00:14:31.237 align:start size:84% position:16%
but feel free to
correct that if not.

00:14:31.237 --> 00:14:33.339
But there's a constant
move to take them out.

00:14:33.339 --> 00:14:35.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, there is a
constant move to make sure

00:14:35.608 --> 00:14:38.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Tucker who is the
number one cable news host

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:42.281 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the history of television
cannot get advertisers.

00:14:42.281 --> 00:14:45.885
How does all that fit into the
situation we're in right now?

00:14:45.885 --> 00:14:47.853
- Remember where that started?

00:14:47.853 --> 00:14:50.322 align:start position:0%
Remember when Barack Obama
tried to take out Rush Limbaugh?

00:14:50.322 --> 00:14:51.257
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:14:51.257 --> 00:14:53.058
- He went after Rush by name.

00:14:53.058 --> 00:14:56.962 align:start position:0%
It was a deliberate White
House effort to take down Rush.

00:14:56.962 --> 00:14:59.031
And then they tried
to do it to Fox.

00:14:59.031 --> 00:15:02.334
And this is why I kind of do
doubt that the mainstream media

00:15:02.334 --> 00:15:04.703
is capable of fixing itself.

00:15:04.703 --> 00:15:06.605
They lost their customers.

00:15:06.605 --> 00:15:11.043
The only reason Fox found a
home was because people voted

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:12.878 align:start size:84% position:16%
to leave the liberal
mainstream media

00:15:12.878 --> 00:15:14.313
'cause they were no
longer being served.

00:15:14.313 --> 00:15:17.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
They recognized how
liberal the media became.

00:15:17.082 --> 00:15:20.185 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it it's kinda like
China with Taiwan.

00:15:20.185 --> 00:15:22.087
The mainstream media
does not like the fact

00:15:22.087 --> 00:15:23.555
that there's a breakaway.

00:15:23.555 --> 00:15:25.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
That there's one group
that thinks independently,

00:15:25.658 --> 00:15:28.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
that thinks differently,
that recognizes that the ways

00:15:28.527 --> 00:15:30.296
of the liberal mainstream media

00:15:30.296 --> 00:15:32.464
are not the ways
of most Americans.

00:15:32.464 --> 00:15:35.301
And so they need
to take down Fox

00:15:35.301 --> 00:15:38.270
just like China wants
to re-absorb Taiwan.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:42.107
And again, look at the ratings.

00:15:42.107 --> 00:15:44.510
The American people
have rejected what
the mainstream media

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:46.612
is doing, that's why
Fox is so popular.

00:15:46.612 --> 00:15:48.080
They struck a niche.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.883
They found a home with the
independent different thought

00:15:50.883 --> 00:15:52.851
and it can't be stopped.

00:15:52.851 --> 00:15:54.553
But Fox is really alone.

00:15:54.553 --> 00:15:57.656
You have Fox, you do have a
talk radio, you do a podcast,

00:15:57.656 --> 00:16:00.426
you do have now have a new
type of electronic front,

00:16:00.426 --> 00:16:03.295 align:start size:84% position:16%
The Federalist, "The
Daily Examiner",
things of that nature,

00:16:03.295 --> 00:16:05.030
The Daily Wire.

00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:08.634
But again, it's the
balkanization of
American information

00:16:08.634 --> 00:16:12.905
and I read left right
and center but man,

00:16:12.905 --> 00:16:15.074
I have to work hard
to figure things out.

00:16:15.074 --> 00:16:17.042 align:start size:97% position:3%
Again, I think it'd be easier
for the American people

00:16:17.042 --> 00:16:18.344
if we had faith in one

00:16:18.344 --> 00:16:20.512
as opposed to have
this balkanization.

00:16:20.512 --> 00:16:22.348
- How much of this do
you think is connected

00:16:22.348 --> 00:16:25.851 align:start size:97% position:3%
to sort of the progressive
takeover of the Democrat Party

00:16:25.851 --> 00:16:29.421
that say when you were press
secretary and as you said,

00:16:29.421 --> 00:16:31.690
there was a little more of a
blend of reporters in there.

00:16:31.690 --> 00:16:34.626
Okay, the answer is different
but we can basically agree

00:16:34.626 --> 00:16:37.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the facts that now
basically the modern left,

00:16:37.997 --> 00:16:39.698 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Democrat Party, they
don't believe America

00:16:39.698 --> 00:16:41.233
is fundamentally decent.

00:16:41.233 --> 00:16:43.302
They don't seem to believe
in our founding documents

00:16:43.302 --> 00:16:45.571 align:start size:88% position:13%
or separation of powers,
all of these things.

00:16:45.571 --> 00:16:48.073
So that of course is then
gonna leak into the people

00:16:48.073 --> 00:16:51.410
who are translating the
news to the average guy.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:52.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And it's even
worse than that, Dave

00:16:52.811 --> 00:16:54.980
because you have a young
generation of reporters

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
who think their job is to
be subjective not objective.

00:16:57.916 --> 00:17:00.185 align:start size:97% position:3%
They think there's something
wrong with objectivity.

00:17:00.185 --> 00:17:02.588
There was a study that
came out just last week.

00:17:02.588 --> 00:17:06.225 align:start size:97% position:3%
It was a Pew study of
journalists and it reported

00:17:06.225 --> 00:17:08.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the majority of
journalists don't believe

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:10.396
in telling two sides
of the news anymore.

00:17:10.396 --> 00:17:12.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
That they view one
side as misinformation,

00:17:12.297 --> 00:17:13.565
it shouldn't be told.

00:17:13.565 --> 00:17:15.601
Well, whose opinion is
it if it's information

00:17:15.601 --> 00:17:17.302
or misinformation?

00:17:17.302 --> 00:17:22.007
And it said that news outlets
whose audiences lean left,

00:17:22.007 --> 00:17:25.377
by two to one, don't think they
should be telling both sides

00:17:25.377 --> 00:17:26.812
of a story.

00:17:26.812 --> 00:17:29.782 align:start position:0%
And then it surveyed journalists
who worked at news outlets

00:17:29.782 --> 00:17:33.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
whose audience leans
right and by them, 60/40,

00:17:33.852 --> 00:17:36.688
it was said that you should
cover two sides of the story.

00:17:36.688 --> 00:17:38.157
So who's being fair?

00:17:38.157 --> 00:17:39.491
Who is being objective?

00:17:39.491 --> 00:17:41.393
And who is being lopsided?

00:17:41.393 --> 00:17:44.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's the takeover
of journalism

00:17:44.696 --> 00:17:47.800 align:start position:0%
by these young social warriors,
which is what they are,

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.803
is killing journalism and it
happened at the New York Times.

00:17:50.803 --> 00:17:53.105
One of my favorite parts of
the book is what happened

00:17:53.105 --> 00:17:56.008 align:start size:88% position:13%
when Senator Tom Cotton
wrote op-ed online

00:17:56.008 --> 00:17:58.510
for the New York Times that
the New York Times headlined

00:17:58.510 --> 00:18:00.179
Send in the Troops.

00:18:00.179 --> 00:18:01.914
And it was in the summer of 2020

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:05.050
after the George Floyd murder
and riots were everywhere.

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:07.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
150 federal buildings
were damaged.

00:18:07.753 --> 00:18:10.656
And Tom Cotton said
Send in the Troops.

00:18:10.656 --> 00:18:13.058
A meltdown in the New
York Times newsroom.

00:18:13.058 --> 00:18:16.295
Young reporters, one after
another after another tweeted

00:18:16.295 --> 00:18:19.364
that this was violence to them.

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:20.799
The violence was in the streets.

00:18:20.799 --> 00:18:22.334 align:start size:84% position:16%
The violence was
with the poor people

00:18:22.334 --> 00:18:24.870
who ran mom and pop shops
that were being torn down

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.872
and looted and destroyed.

00:18:26.872 --> 00:18:29.675 align:start position:0%
But it was these people sitting
in a cubicle in Manhattan

00:18:29.675 --> 00:18:32.177 align:start size:88% position:13%
who said, this is
violence and they forced

00:18:32.177 --> 00:18:35.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
The New York Times to tuck its
tail, to retract the op-ed,

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:37.716
to say it never should have
been run in the first place,

00:18:37.716 --> 00:18:39.651 align:start size:84% position:16%
to fire their
editorial page editor,

00:18:39.651 --> 00:18:41.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
reassign the deputy
editorial page editor.

00:18:41.987 --> 00:18:43.689
It was a purge.

00:18:43.689 --> 00:18:46.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was like a college
campus broke out

00:18:46.191 --> 00:18:48.193
inside The New York Times
where the young people

00:18:48.193 --> 00:18:50.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
demanded the administration
silence of voice

00:18:50.762 --> 00:18:52.698
with which they disagree.

00:18:52.698 --> 00:18:55.334
It was one of the worst,
most embarrassing episodes

00:18:55.334 --> 00:18:58.704
to be a media organization
dedicated to free speech.

00:18:58.704 --> 00:19:00.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
But free speech
didn't stand a chance

00:19:00.706 --> 00:19:03.075
against the young social
warriors of journalism.

00:19:03.075 --> 00:19:05.844
- Yeah and that's also
why for me personally,

00:19:05.844 --> 00:19:07.479
to me these things
have been infected.

00:19:07.479 --> 00:19:09.748
I don't think CNN can come back.

00:19:09.748 --> 00:19:13.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
When I give talks at college
campuses, if I say CNN,

00:19:13.085 --> 00:19:15.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
I used to say CNN and
then I would make a joke.

00:19:15.554 --> 00:19:17.890
Now if I just say
CNN, they laugh.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:19.691
CNN is already to the punchline

00:19:19.691 --> 00:19:22.461 align:start position:0%
just saying those three letters
is already the punchline.

00:19:22.461 --> 00:19:23.729
So something like
The New York times,

00:19:23.729 --> 00:19:26.665
I mean, Barry Weiss
is not liberal enough

00:19:26.665 --> 00:19:27.799
for the New York Times.

00:19:27.799 --> 00:19:29.935 align:start size:88% position:13%
They got a real
problem on their hands.

00:19:29.935 --> 00:19:32.938
- Exactly right and
all newsrooms do

00:19:32.938 --> 00:19:35.240 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I interviewed
one journalism professor

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:38.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
at the Cronkite School in
Arizona and she was telling me

00:19:38.343 --> 00:19:41.180
that many of her students
really do heartedly believe

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:43.182
that the reason you
go into journalism

00:19:43.182 --> 00:19:46.051
is to bring your point
of view to the people

00:19:46.051 --> 00:19:48.854
as opposed to bring facts
and the truth to the people

00:19:48.854 --> 00:19:51.390
and let the people tell you
what their point of view is.

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:53.025
And Dave, that's the
fundamental point.

00:19:53.025 --> 00:19:56.328
Newspapers have analyses,
newspapers have commentary.

00:19:56.328 --> 00:19:58.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
If it's on the editorial page,
if it's on the nightly part

00:19:58.964 --> 00:20:01.967
of the Fox News hour with
Sean Hannity or with Tucker

00:20:01.967 --> 00:20:06.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
or with Don Lemon at CNN,
that's opinion and we get it.

00:20:06.205 --> 00:20:10.509
But when the news itself
is twisted to represent

00:20:10.509 --> 00:20:12.744
a point of view, we
have a real problem.

00:20:12.744 --> 00:20:15.547
And that's been the case for
decades at The New York Times.

00:20:15.547 --> 00:20:17.716
And infection is a good
word, it's infected,

00:20:17.716 --> 00:20:19.718
the mainstream media
all over the place.

00:20:19.718 --> 00:20:22.554
That's why I keep blowing a
whistle throughout this book.

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:25.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, one of the
favorite things I do in here

00:20:25.257 --> 00:20:28.093
is I put pictures up of
how The New York Times

00:20:28.093 --> 00:20:29.861
covered the exact same thing.

00:20:29.861 --> 00:20:33.765
So when Ruth Bader
Ginsburg passed away,

00:20:33.765 --> 00:20:35.767
front page headline, this
was The Washington Post,

00:20:35.767 --> 00:20:40.739 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the front page headline
was Pioneer for Equality Dies.

00:20:42.207 --> 00:20:44.776
When Antonin Scalia passed
away, the same headline,

00:20:44.776 --> 00:20:48.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
Washington Post, banner
headline was something along

00:20:48.847 --> 00:20:53.819
the lines of the Champion
of Liberal Taunting Dies.

00:20:55.687 --> 00:20:57.389
Something along those lines.

00:20:57.389 --> 00:21:01.593
One was a pioneer for rights,
the other taunted the left,

00:21:01.593 --> 00:21:02.995
how is this fair?

00:21:02.995 --> 00:21:04.062
How is this neutral?

00:21:04.062 --> 00:21:05.564
How is it objective?

00:21:05.564 --> 00:21:08.834
But it's a point of view and
it's in the papers every day.

00:21:08.834 --> 00:21:11.670
- Does that also explain why
we sort of seemingly have like

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:13.505 align:start size:84% position:16%
a mass mental health
problem right now

00:21:13.505 --> 00:21:16.942
that so many people have
bought into this framework

00:21:16.942 --> 00:21:20.212 align:start position:0%
that is dishonest and it doesn't
connect with what they see

00:21:20.212 --> 00:21:22.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
in real life but it's
constantly pushed on them,

00:21:22.547 --> 00:21:25.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
so everyone starts feeling
crazy about politics?

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:27.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
- One of the other points
I really make in here

00:21:27.085 --> 00:21:29.388
that I think is different is
we all know what it's like

00:21:29.388 --> 00:21:31.957
to be a conservative and
have them look down on us

00:21:31.957 --> 00:21:33.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
and treat us with
disdain for our habits

00:21:33.825 --> 00:21:36.061
or views or traditions.

00:21:36.061 --> 00:21:39.931
But the group that I also argue
is really hurt are liberals.

00:21:39.931 --> 00:21:42.668 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because if everything you
see, if everything you're told

00:21:42.668 --> 00:21:44.636
and if all the people you're
surrounded with say to you,

00:21:44.636 --> 00:21:46.138
Hillary's going to win.

00:21:46.138 --> 00:21:48.640
It's going to be a landslide,
of course she's gonna win.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.842
Trump has zero chance.

00:21:50.842 --> 00:21:52.010
And then Trump wins.

00:21:53.478 --> 00:21:55.247 line:0%
You either have to say,
how come you misinform me

00:21:55.247 --> 00:21:56.214 line:0%
which they don't do?

00:21:56.214 --> 00:21:57.716 line:0%
Or how did Trump do it?

00:21:57.716 --> 00:22:00.285 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
It had to be another reason,
it had to be collusion.

00:22:00.285 --> 00:22:03.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you then are susceptible
to people giving you

00:22:03.021 --> 00:22:05.757
the misinformation
that Trump colluded.

00:22:05.757 --> 00:22:07.859
And then when you hear
Mueller is investigating

00:22:07.859 --> 00:22:10.862 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you watch MSNBC,
you're told every night,

00:22:10.862 --> 00:22:14.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
the walls are closing
in, another shoe dropped.

00:22:15.767 --> 00:22:19.871
More questions than answers
and you reach the conclusion,

00:22:19.871 --> 00:22:21.139
Trump's on his way out.

00:22:21.139 --> 00:22:23.008
You do it now with the
January 6th Committee.

00:22:23.008 --> 00:22:24.843
Trump's gonna get
indicted, isn't he?

00:22:24.843 --> 00:22:27.713
These people keep fooling
themselves into going down

00:22:27.713 --> 00:22:30.315
a criminal cul-de-sac that
says the people I don't like

00:22:30.315 --> 00:22:31.950
in politics are criminals

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:33.885
and somebody's about
to get rid of them.

00:22:33.885 --> 00:22:37.155 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when it doesn't happen,
you can't understand it

00:22:37.155 --> 00:22:39.791
'cause everybody thinks
the same way you do.

00:22:39.791 --> 00:22:42.627 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I do think that
contributes to this feeling

00:22:42.627 --> 00:22:45.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
that people have of
bitterness, of animosity,

00:22:45.697 --> 00:22:48.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
of mental breakdowns
in some cases

00:22:48.667 --> 00:22:53.138
because what they were told to
expect doesn't match reality.

00:22:53.138 --> 00:22:54.773
And that's the problem
with the liberal media,

00:22:54.773 --> 00:22:57.442
it's hurting the liberal
base of our country too.

00:22:57.442 --> 00:22:59.111 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So I think most
people watching this

00:22:59.111 --> 00:23:01.980 align:start size:84% position:16%
get sort of the young
journalist, quote
unquote journalist,

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:03.415
I always have to put air
quotes with journalists

00:23:03.415 --> 00:23:05.617
but the young kid part
of it that they come in

00:23:05.617 --> 00:23:06.852
and infect these things.

00:23:06.852 --> 00:23:08.286
But what do you think
at the corporate level?

00:23:08.286 --> 00:23:10.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because when we show
clips of MSNBC on my show

00:23:10.655 --> 00:23:12.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
which we do a
couple times a week,

00:23:12.557 --> 00:23:15.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
I call it the televised
mental institution of MSNBC

00:23:15.594 --> 00:23:17.562
because you would
not be able to tell

00:23:17.562 --> 00:23:20.031
that it was not broadcasting
from a mental institution.

00:23:20.031 --> 00:23:22.234 align:start size:88% position:13%
When you listen to some
of the crazy things

00:23:22.234 --> 00:23:23.468
that Joy Reid is saying.

00:23:23.468 --> 00:23:27.305
I mean, it genuinely, it
seems psychotic to me,

00:23:27.305 --> 00:23:30.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
that would be my
prescription on that.

00:23:30.242 --> 00:23:32.144 align:start size:84% position:16%
But what do you think
is happening at the
corporate level?

00:23:32.144 --> 00:23:33.678
Like I get at the
newsroom level,

00:23:33.678 --> 00:23:36.915
okay, maybe they've been all
infected but this is MSNBC,

00:23:36.915 --> 00:23:40.619
Microsoft NBC, these are
massive corporate powers

00:23:40.619 --> 00:23:41.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they're
watching this going,

00:23:41.753 --> 00:23:43.288
they know this can't be true,

00:23:43.288 --> 00:23:45.724
that this nonsense is not
true that they're spewing.

00:23:45.724 --> 00:23:48.760
- As long as it appeals to
a significant enough niche,

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:51.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
they can make enough money
on advertising revenue

00:23:51.096 --> 00:23:54.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
and subscribers,
that's all they need.

00:23:54.433 --> 00:23:57.102 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so they've given up the
notion of serving the country,

00:23:57.102 --> 00:24:00.105
serving all the people, they
just need to serve their niche.

00:24:00.105 --> 00:24:03.508
And if their niche wants to
hear that Trump colluded,

00:24:03.508 --> 00:24:06.778 align:start size:81% position:19%
if their niche
wants to hear that

00:24:06.778 --> 00:24:09.181
Stacey Abrams actual
election was stolen,

00:24:09.181 --> 00:24:11.383
that's what they'll
tell their niche.

00:24:11.383 --> 00:24:13.885
So again, this is technology.

00:24:13.885 --> 00:24:16.621
You know, in the older days,
when you didn't really have

00:24:16.621 --> 00:24:18.723 align:start size:88% position:13%
all these niches that
were so easily available

00:24:18.723 --> 00:24:20.425
to be found on the internet,

00:24:20.425 --> 00:24:24.729
you had to tell the news in
a broader, more fair sense.

00:24:24.729 --> 00:24:26.731
They don't have to do that
anymore so that's again,

00:24:26.731 --> 00:24:28.233
reason for pessimism.

00:24:28.233 --> 00:24:31.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
But here's how these
things ultimately do fix.

00:24:31.203 --> 00:24:33.071 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know one of the
reasons a Hispanic vote

00:24:33.071 --> 00:24:34.739
is increasingly
voting Republican

00:24:35.941 --> 00:24:39.144
is because of all this
woke stuff, all this.

00:24:39.144 --> 00:24:41.146
You know, men can have babies?

00:24:41.146 --> 00:24:45.050
If you had any bit
of traditional in
you, you'll just say,

00:24:45.050 --> 00:24:46.451
who are these people?

00:24:46.451 --> 00:24:48.753
I can't be part
of this thinking.

00:24:48.753 --> 00:24:51.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
And Hispanics are increasingly
turning Republican.

00:24:51.623 --> 00:24:53.692 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think one of the solutions
here is the Democrats

00:24:53.692 --> 00:24:55.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
start to lose their
base of support

00:24:55.660 --> 00:25:00.031 align:start size:97% position:3%
and increasingly just remain,
in MSNBC, just remains

00:25:00.031 --> 00:25:04.970
the tellers of tales for upper
income, liberal white people

00:25:06.404 --> 00:25:07.706
and they've lost working
class blue collar people.

00:25:07.706 --> 00:25:09.574
They've started to
lose Hispanic voters

00:25:09.574 --> 00:25:11.476
and if they start to
lose African Americans

00:25:11.476 --> 00:25:13.278
more than they already are,

00:25:13.278 --> 00:25:15.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's when they're
gonna start to realize

00:25:15.413 --> 00:25:18.116
this upper middle
income white niche,

00:25:18.116 --> 00:25:20.452
the upper west side
isn't big enough.

00:25:20.452 --> 00:25:23.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
We need to change,
that's when it'll happen.

00:25:23.088 --> 00:25:26.725
- So since I have no doubt you
know basically everyone in DC

00:25:26.725 --> 00:25:30.295 align:start position:0%
although maybe some of them
won't talk to you publicly like,

00:25:30.295 --> 00:25:32.731 align:start size:97% position:3%
do you talk to any Democrats,
old school Democrats,

00:25:32.731 --> 00:25:35.233
say guys that were across the
aisle from you 20 years ago

00:25:35.233 --> 00:25:37.502 align:start size:97% position:3%
when you were in the
administration that are going,

00:25:37.502 --> 00:25:40.038 align:start size:97% position:3%
we just lost control of this
thing like that the train

00:25:40.038 --> 00:25:41.473 align:start size:81% position:19%
is just completely
outta control.

00:25:41.473 --> 00:25:43.275
We don't know how to
stop what's going on

00:25:43.275 --> 00:25:44.543
with the Democrats right now.

00:25:44.543 --> 00:25:46.444
- Yeah, I think there's
a growing sense of that.

00:25:46.444 --> 00:25:49.481 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it takes elections,
it really takes

00:25:49.481 --> 00:25:52.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
when a group that you
always had in your corner

00:25:52.284 --> 00:25:55.787 align:start size:94% position:6%
like the Hispanic vote
does start to break 50/50.

00:25:55.787 --> 00:25:57.789
These are the things
that shock politicians

00:25:57.789 --> 00:25:59.324
into changing their behavior.

00:25:59.324 --> 00:26:01.226
Now I think Joe Manchin is
on the front edge of that

00:26:01.226 --> 00:26:02.761
for the Democratic Party.

00:26:02.761 --> 00:26:05.830
Kyrsten Sinema, interestingly
as well, Senator from Arizona.

00:26:07.232 --> 00:26:09.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's the Bernie Sanders,
the Elizabeth Warrens

00:26:09.701 --> 00:26:11.202
that are killing the party.

00:26:11.202 --> 00:26:13.538
And you'll notice
it's the appeal

00:26:13.538 --> 00:26:16.308 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the upper income,
white liberal.

00:26:16.308 --> 00:26:19.678
And that's just the
wrong group to create

00:26:19.678 --> 00:26:23.715 align:start size:84% position:16%
any type of national
consensus from.

00:26:23.715 --> 00:26:26.851 align:start size:97% position:3%
And one of the great things
about American politics

00:26:26.851 --> 00:26:30.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
is we have to create broad
coalitions to govern.

00:26:30.522 --> 00:26:33.525 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're not like Europe, we
don't have six major parties

00:26:33.525 --> 00:26:35.327
in our parliaments
each of which splits

00:26:35.327 --> 00:26:37.963 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then you have to
play coalition politics.

00:26:37.963 --> 00:26:41.700
We have two big parties and
if two big parties can create

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:44.302 align:start size:88% position:13%
internal coalitions,
they can govern America.

00:26:44.302 --> 00:26:45.971
If Democrats are on
their way to proving,

00:26:45.971 --> 00:26:47.405
they're proving it under by them

00:26:47.405 --> 00:26:48.873
that they can't govern America.

00:26:48.873 --> 00:26:50.308
- So I wanna get a
little more into that

00:26:50.308 --> 00:26:52.410
but going a bit more
on the media side,

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.346 align:start position:0%
you've mentioned this
balkanization a couple of times

00:26:54.346 --> 00:26:55.413
and that there's a danger there.

00:26:55.413 --> 00:26:56.581
Do you think in some ways that

00:26:56.581 --> 00:27:00.285 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's also a
silver lining to it

00:27:00.285 --> 00:27:02.988
which is that the more
that we get balkanized,

00:27:02.988 --> 00:27:04.623
whether it's geographically,

00:27:04.623 --> 00:27:07.492
as you know I moved
from Cali to Florida.

00:27:07.492 --> 00:27:08.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
Or through the
information that we get,

00:27:08.994 --> 00:27:13.098 align:start position:0%
that people are really thinking
about what they believe,

00:27:13.098 --> 00:27:14.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they are
actually putting it,

00:27:14.399 --> 00:27:16.101 align:start size:81% position:19%
some of this stuff
into practice,

00:27:16.101 --> 00:27:18.536 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether it's by moving
or finding sources

00:27:18.536 --> 00:27:21.206 align:start size:97% position:3%
that make sense to them and
ignoring some of the old stuff

00:27:21.206 --> 00:27:23.875
and that we just are
in a transition phase.

00:27:23.875 --> 00:27:26.745
It just is and there
are more choices now.

00:27:26.745 --> 00:27:28.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah and the fact is
the pendulum always swings

00:27:28.747 --> 00:27:31.449
in the United States and
it goes in one direction

00:27:31.449 --> 00:27:34.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a while and goes
back in another direction

00:27:34.819 --> 00:27:37.122
and it never swings that far.

00:27:37.122 --> 00:27:40.091
And that's one of the great
strengths of our country.

00:27:40.091 --> 00:27:41.359
So I think there's
something to that

00:27:41.359 --> 00:27:43.662
and I also look at the
history of journalism.

00:27:43.662 --> 00:27:48.066
We're back to the way
we were on 1776 or 1789

00:27:48.066 --> 00:27:51.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
when pamphlets really
represented news.

00:27:51.102 --> 00:27:52.804
And my side put out a pamphlet,

00:27:52.804 --> 00:27:54.305 align:start size:81% position:19%
the other side put
out a pamphlet.

00:27:54.305 --> 00:27:55.473
It was all advocacy.

00:27:56.908 --> 00:27:59.477
This notion of objective
reporting is a relatively

00:27:59.477 --> 00:28:02.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
newfound notion that began
the early to middle on

00:28:02.614 --> 00:28:04.749
part of the 20th century.

00:28:04.749 --> 00:28:06.518
Prior to that, you really
had yellow journalism,

00:28:06.518 --> 00:28:10.822 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Hearsts and scare
everybody as much as you can

00:28:10.822 --> 00:28:14.159
and sell the paper for
a penny every afternoon.

00:28:14.159 --> 00:28:17.796
So things do swing and
change but eventually

00:28:17.796 --> 00:28:22.167
people get fed up and things
go back to what is sensible.

00:28:22.167 --> 00:28:24.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's why I'm hoping
it'll return to what sensible,

00:28:24.469 --> 00:28:25.637
that's why I wrote the book.

00:28:25.637 --> 00:28:27.272 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's why I have my
recommendations in there

00:28:27.272 --> 00:28:29.941 align:start size:97% position:3%
for how to change newsrooms
and journalism schools.

00:28:31.342 --> 00:28:33.278
And if it doesn't happen in the
next five years or 10 years,

00:28:33.278 --> 00:28:36.481
I think it's still going to
happen because eventually

00:28:36.481 --> 00:28:39.350 align:start position:0%
that pendulum always heads
back to the sensible direction.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:42.587 align:start position:0%
- So a lot of people always
ask me, was it always this bad?

00:28:42.587 --> 00:28:46.091
And I try to think
back, I'm I'm 46.

00:28:46.091 --> 00:28:49.828
I remember the nightly
news on ABC, CBS, NBC

00:28:49.828 --> 00:28:52.263
and it was basically
whether you liked Broca

00:28:52.263 --> 00:28:56.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
or Peter Jennings or what's
his name? Dan Rather.

00:28:56.634 --> 00:28:59.404
But that the structure of the
show and what they covered

00:28:59.404 --> 00:29:01.239
was basically the same.

00:29:02.741 --> 00:29:04.342
Maybe the order was a
little bit different

00:29:04.342 --> 00:29:05.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
but they pretty much
recovering the same things

00:29:05.977 --> 00:29:07.479
or Walter Cronkite before that.

00:29:07.479 --> 00:29:11.683
Do you think there was too
much protection over topics

00:29:11.683 --> 00:29:13.718
that maybe they weren't
talking about back then?

00:29:13.718 --> 00:29:15.353
- Well, protection over topics,

00:29:15.353 --> 00:29:19.290
what I would say is there
was too much of a one sided

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:21.359
nature of storytelling.

00:29:21.359 --> 00:29:24.896
How often would you see on
the news about a good guy

00:29:24.896 --> 00:29:27.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
